A||||3||ThatRickGuy||Temp guage, lights, action!!||||11||
Z||000000||ThatRickGuy||11-03-2000||08:15 PM||||Heya people, ran into a little problem... starting off, my temp guage has never worked, in never pegged when you start the car or anything, just always sits at 0. so, looking for a way to be safe and avoid overheating, I decided to do the idiot light rewire. so I did the whole dark green wire/2 light green wire deal. I also pulled apart the dash to make sure all the connections on the temp guage were good. Then when I put it all back together, things got screwy. Now, the idiot light doesn't turn on, the temp guage pegs high then just sits (not stuck, just no juice) and when ever I turn my high beams on the headlights go out (parking lights stay on and lights stay in up position, but no light comes out) Any ideas?!?! are the brights some how wired in series w/ the dash bright light, so that a dead dash light could make the brights go out? the car is an '88 formula just incase there is any difference in wiring accross the years. Thanks.||||reg||11
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||11-03-2000||11:28 PM||||Rick,<P>Studied the schematics for the cluster, gauges, warning lamps and high beam indicator.<BR>Are you sure that you didn't get pins 11 & 12 confused? 11 lt. green - coolant (sensor side), 12 lt. green - high beam indicator, 13 dk green - temp idiot light. I'm not sure what it was that you were trying to accomplish by this but it's fairly easy to see that now the 'bulb test' on start is routed through the gauge, not sure what happened to the lt. green wire that went to the gauge, unless it now goes to the high beams thus, when you turn on the brights -NO JUICE! You then must be back feeding the temp gauge circut with +12 volts, ie. nothing happens. The high beam indicator light has no  associated function with the opperation of the lights at all, it just lets you know when they're on.<BR>Undo what you have done and things will be back the way they were. If you want the temp gauge to work, trouble shoot the circut and the sensor (now fried from the +12 volts back feed).<p>[This message has been edited by Indiana_resto_guy (edited 11-03-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||ThatRickGuy||11-04-2000||10:31 AM||||okay, well, I got it back to were it was, I had the right idiot light wire, but the dual light greens off the inj connection were the problem, after checking the lights again, when ever I hit the bright switch, the light would die and the idiot temp light would come on, so I'm guessing that the schematic must be slightly off for '88 electronics. so I unwired every thing to get it to work correctly. by that time tho, it was getting late, and working under the light of a street light (T-tops help visibility a lot!) I was to tired to go back in and try to figure out the correct wires. on an interesting note tho, I did the short test on the sensor and the idiot light works, and the guage pegged, but it doesn't peg on start up nor does it move w/ the temperature... ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Indiana_resto_guy||11-04-2000||01:38 PM||||Do you know anyone with a scan tool? If you do plug it in and see if the tem sensor for the gauge is working. Did you do a ohms check on the gauge or the correct sensor?||||reg||1
